 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of storage. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The registry stores the active snapshot as a local embedded SQLite database on the kiosk, opened in-process with no separate service. Each record is stored alongside a normalized lookup key (surrounding whitespace trimmed, ASCII letters uppercased, serial punctuation preserved) carried in an indexed column, so a lookup is a single indexed query. Snapshot validation, index build, and lookup all belong to this node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using the bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the new snapshot into a staging SQLite database — records plus their normalized lookup keys — separate from the active one. Activate the new snapshot only after signature, schema, uniqueness, and index build all succeed, by atomically replacing the active database. Any import failure — including a failed index build — leaves the previous database active and reports a useful error. A partially built database is never queried.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize the requested serial by trimming surrounding whitespace and uppercasing ASCII letters; serial punctuation remains significant. Query the active database by the normalized key. Return the active snapshot date in every result, including when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite database built on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Both alternatives require runtime network access, which conflicts with the offline tenets (no runtime network access; kiosks have no network route). SQLite runs in-process with no service and gives predictable local lookups within the 250 ms / 90,000-record goal. The accepted cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on every snapshot import. |
+| Snapshot activation | Build a staging database, then atomically replace the active one | Rebuild in place inside the live database within one transaction | Staging-then-replace keeps the last valid database untouched until the new one is complete, satisfying "a failed import leaves the last valid snapshot available" even across a crash mid-build. In-place rebuild risks leaving the live database mid-rebuild and holds the live file locked throughout. The cost is transient disk room for two databases. |
+| Normalization storage | Store a normalized, indexed lookup key beside the raw serial | Normalize at query time against raw serials | A stored indexed key keeps lookups to one indexed query under the latency goal; query-time normalization forces a scan or a function index. Punctuation is preserved in the key so it stays significant. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index-build failure (disk full, I/O error) is presently only implied by the general "import failure retains the active snapshot" behavior; whether it warrants its own EARS line is for the spec phase.
+2. The disk headroom needed to hold the active and staging databases together for a 150 MB snapshot is unquantified.
+3. Cold start: what is available if the first-ever import fails, with no previous snapshot to retain.
